[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **No mismatches were found.** Every input class enumerated below produced
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte-identical stdout, byte-identical stderr (always empty) and exit status 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m from both programs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Coverage behind that claim:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `translation/tests/differential.rs` — 23 tests, ~250 distinct inputs, all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   passing under both `cargo test` and `cargo test --release`, none `#[ignore]`d.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Two ad-hoc differential fuzz runs outside the suite (4,643 and 23,000 inputs)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   over random byte strings and random wide-range numeric literals: 0 mismatches.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   The interesting cases from those runs are folded into the suite, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `deterministic_sweep` reproduces a 600-input slice with a fixed seed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m To make sure the harness is capable of failing, it was pointed at a deliberately
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m wrong program (`RUST_DRIVER_BIN=/bin/cat`): 22 of 23 tests failed, the only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m survivor being the `c_src` content-hash guard, which does not run either binary.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A suite that cannot fail proves nothing, so this negative control is part of the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m evidence.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## What the C program branches on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The whole program is:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int x = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m scanf("%d", &x);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m driver(x);          // sets bedrooms = 3, bathrooms = 2.0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m                     // then hex-dumps the 16-byte struct image
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m return 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `driver` and `print_hex` are unconditional — no `if`, no early `return`, no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m length or null check anywhere. The single input-dependent decision is the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m outcome of `scanf("%d", &x)`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | outcome | trigger | effect |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | successful conversion | optional whitespace, optional sign, ≥1 digit | `x` = converted value |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | matching failure | first non-whitespace byte cannot start an integer; or EOF right after a sign | `x` keeps `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | input failure | EOF (or a read error) before any non-whitespace byte | `x` keeps `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Because `x` is serialized byte-for-byte into the output, *every distinct
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m converted value is its own observable case*, which is why the tests sweep bit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m patterns and both `int` and `long` boundaries rather than a handful of values.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Baseline output, for reference: `printf("%02x")` over the 16-byte image plus one
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"\n"`. For input `1` that is `01000000030000000000000000000040\n` —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `floors=1` (LE `int`), `bedrooms=3` (LE `int`), `bathrooms=2.0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (LE IEEE-754 double `0x4000000000000000`). No padding holes on x86-64 LP64, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `house_t house = {0}` zeroes the object anyway, so no indeterminate bytes are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m printed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Hazards checked, and why each one is already right
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the places a naive translation would have diverged. Each was verified
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m against the C binary rather than reasoned about alone.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`scanf` skips whitespace across newlines.** `%d` consumes a run of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `isspace()` characters before the number, so `"\n\n\n42"` yields 42, not a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    failure. A `read_line`-based translation would have produced 0 here. The Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    code implements the skip explicitly over `' ' \t \n \v \f \r`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Test: `scanf_skips_leading_whitespace_across_newlines`, `whitespace_only_input`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **Failed conversion leaves `x` at its previous value.** `scanf` does not zero
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the target on failure; the C code happens to have initialized `x = 0`, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    observable value is 0. The Rust `main` only assigns on `Some(..)`, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    dependency on the initializer is preserved rather than accidentally
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    hard-coded. Test: `matching_failure_leaves_x_zero`, `empty_input`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **Overflow saturates, then truncates.** glibc's `%d` converts with `strtol`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    into a `long`, clamping to `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`, then stores through an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `int *`, truncating to 32 bits. The two-stage behavior is visible and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    distinguishes clamping from wrapping:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    - `9223372036854775808` → clamped to `LONG_MAX` → low 32 bits all ones →
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      `ffffffff…`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    - `-99999999999999999999999` → clamped to `LONG_MIN` → low 32 bits zero →
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      `00000000…`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    - `4294967296` → no clamp, truncates to 0 → `00000000…`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    A translation using `i32::from_str` would have failed the conversion outright
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and printed 0 for the first case. One using wrapping accumulation would have
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    printed something else again. Both effects are reproduced in `scanf_d`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    (saturating `i64` accumulation, then `as i32`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Tests: `values_beyond_int_are_truncated_like_c`, `long_boundaries_and_overflow`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. **Digit runs longer than any buffer.** 100,000 `'9'`s, 100,000 leading `'0'`s
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    followed by `5`, and 100,000 spaces before a value all match. The saturating
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    accumulator does not care how long the run is, and leading zeros never trip
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    saturation. Test: `very_long_digit_runs`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5. **Conversion stops at the first byte that cannot extend the number, and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    rest of stdin is never read.** `"0x1f"` is 0 (stops at `x`), `"1.5"` is 1,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `"1 2"` is 1. Tests: `conversion_stops_at_first_non_digit`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `only_first_item_is_read`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 6. **A lone sign, or a sign followed by whitespace or EOF, is a matching
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    failure.** `"-"`, `"+"`, `"- 5"`, `"--5"`, `"+-5"` all yield 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Test: `matching_failure_leaves_x_zero`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 7. **Raw bytes.** NUL and `0xff` are neither whitespace nor digits, so they are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    matching failures in leading position and terminators after digits. The Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    reader works on bytes, not `char`s, so invalid UTF-8 on stdin cannot make it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    diverge or panic — a `String`-based translation would have. Test:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `non_ascii_and_nul_bytes`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 8. **Read errors are not input.** With a directory on stdin (`EISDIR`) or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `/dev/null`, both programs print the `x = 0` image and exit 0; neither writes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    to stderr. Tests: `stdin_is_a_directory`, `stdin_closed`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 9. **Buffering does not change the bytes.** `printf` is line-buffered to a pipe
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and fully buffered to a file; the Rust side uses a `BufWriter` with an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    explicit final flush. Output is identical in both cases, and a dropped flush
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    would show up as empty stdout. Test: `stdout_to_a_file_matches`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 10. **`argv` is ignored.** C `main()` takes no parameters, so arguments must not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     change anything. Test: `command_line_arguments_are_ignored`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 11. **Struct layout.** Field order, offsets (`0`, `4`, `8`), little-endian
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     encoding and the `2.0` double bit pattern are all exercised: 31 powers of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     two, their negatives, and masks like `0x0102_0304` and `0x00ff_00ff` make
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     every byte of the `floors` field non-zero in some case, so a wrong offset or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     a byte-order error could not hide. Tests:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     `every_byte_of_the_value_is_exercised`, `reference_output_shape_is_pinned`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Limits of this verification
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Points 3 and 4 depend on glibc's `scanf` implementation. The C standard leaves
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `%d` overflow undefined, so a different libc could legitimately behave
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   differently; the translation matches the C program *as built here*, which is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the stated contract.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Struct layout assumes the x86-64 System V ABI (LP64, 8-byte-aligned
